S20 P3 — bidirectionality verification log (ticket #102, epic #98)
Frozen contract: features/propose-grows-up/p3-brainstorm.md (v12)

METHOD
------
A test that passes while the code is present proves nothing on its own — it
may be asserting something the code does not decide. So the production
behaviors this phase adds were verified in BOTH directions: green with the
code present, and RED with the code deliberately broken.

For each behavior below the harness, run from a CLEAN COMMITTED tree:
  1. asserts the mutation string occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the target file
     (so the break is precise and cannot silently miss);
  2. applies that one mutation;
  3. runs the naming test file under vitest and records the genuine failing
     test names, verbatim;
  4. restores with `git checkout -- <file>`.
After the last entry the whole suite is re-run and `git status` is asserted
clean, so nothing below leaked into the tree. (Step 4 restores to HEAD, so
the harness must only ever be run against committed work — running it over
an uncommitted edit silently reverts that edit.)

The harness is reproducible: scratchpad/bidi.sh + scratchpad/mutate.py.

RESULT: 43 behaviors exercised — 41 are PINNED (breaking them fails at least
one named test) and 2 are CONFIRMED BEHAVIOURAL NO-OPS, recorded with their
proofs below rather than given a phantom assertion. No behavior is unpinned.

SCOPE OF THE CLAIM — what this log does NOT cover
-------------------------------------------------
The "verified in both directions" claim above is deliberately scoped to the
behaviors LISTED BELOW; it is not a universal claim over the diff. Three
kinds of change in this phase are outside it:

  a. PRESENTATION — the TIER select's CSS class and the removal of its
     aria-label. The repo has no CSS or accessibility-tree test harness, so
     these were verified by reading the cascade (the class had collided with
     the preview rows' depth marker, whose later equal-specificity rule won)
     rather than by mutation.
  b. STRUCTURAL guards — the two-pass ordering row ("the schematic group may
     come FIRST"). It guards a shape, not a line: no single-line mutation
     expresses the single-pass alternative, so the row's bite is against a
     future refactor that resolves refs during the group walk, and it is not
     listed as a mutation entry below.
  c. COMMENTS AND DOC CITATIONS.

An earlier revision of this file claimed "every production behavior … in
BOTH directions" without these carve-outs, which was broader than the
evidence. Corrected here.

TWO CONFIRMED NO-OPS (deliberate, not gaps)
-------------------------------------------
(1) SEAM :441 — `recipeLabel`'s catalog argument in the CONSTRAINED row.
    The frozen spec predicts this and forbids a pin for it; the sweep
    confirms the prediction empirically. `recipeLabel` uses its catalog for
    one thing, `dflt = effectiveDefaultRecipe(catalog, …)`, and that site
    executes only inside `constrainedRaws.map`. A row is constrained exactly
    when `effectiveDefaultRecipe(gated, …) === null`, so under correct wiring
    no option can carry `(default)`. Under the missed edit `dflt` is the
    ungated default D — but constrained implies D is gated out, and the
    options come from `producerRecipesFor(gated, …)`, so D is absent from
    them and the tag still never fires. The site is changed for consistency;
    the `(default)` tag is pinned where it CAN fire, on a normal stage row.

(2) The TIER select's `value` binding
    (`tierOptions.includes(t) ? String(t) : ""`).
    MEASURED: no client-DOM assertion can discriminate it. Rendering the same
    select with `value=""` and with `value="999"` (no matching option) yields
    a byte-identical DOM — value "", selectedIndex 0, per-option `selected`
    flags [true,false,false], identical innerHTML — because React flags
    `option.selected` rather than setting `select.value`, and an unmatched
    value leaves the DOM's own "ask for a reset" algorithm to select the
    first option, which IS "all". The difference is real only in React's
    SERVER renderer, which this client-rendered SPA never takes. The binding
    is kept (it is the specced shape and keeps a server-rendered string
    honest) and the user-visible outcome — an above-range tier displays
    "all", with no write-back — IS pinned; only the expression is unpinnable,
    and no test pretends otherwise. This finding produced spec v12.

